def npn-switch
  title "Low-side NPN switch with flyback diode"
  about "An S8050 switches a load to ground: 1 kΩ base resistor from the signal, 10 kΩ from base to emitter so that it
      stays off while the firmware is not running, and an SS14 across the load for coils. The buzzer stage of the
      reference project; up to about 100 mA saturated from a 3.3 V pin."
  port in "the driving signal (GPIO)"
  port load "the low side of the load (relay coil, buzzer, LED string)"
  port vplus "the supply of the load (its high side)"
  port gnd "ground"
  part Q1 transistor "S8050 J3Y" lcsc C2146 "{as}: low-side switch"
  part R1 resistor 0805W8F1001T5E lcsc C17513 value 1k "{as}: base resistor 1 k"
  part R2 resistor 0805W8F1002T5E lcsc C17414 value 10k "{as}: base-emitter resistor 10 k"
  part D1 diode SS14 lcsc C2480 "{as}: flyback diode"
  net in R1.1
  net base 0..900 mV Q1.base R1.2 R2.1
  net load @vplus Q1.collector D1.anode
  net vplus D1.cathode
  net gnd Q1.emitter R2.2
  covers LVL.undefined REQ.missing HINT.inductive
  notes "The load goes between vplus and load: add it to the graph with its pins on these two nets. With a series
      resistor in front of a coil, vplus is the coil's own plus terminal, so that the diode sits directly across the
      coil."
  notes "Base current from 3.3 V about 2.4 mA: saturated up to about 100 mA (forced beta 40, hFE at least 200). For
      more current a smaller base resistor or a MOSFET; edakit sim shows VCE and the power (LIM)."
  notes "sim.json: the driving net under the MCU's outputs."
  notes "D1 is optional: for a coil it takes the flyback current; for LEDs or a resistive load `--without D1` leaves
      it out."
  optional parts D1
